{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyCjRJGvcfyqLnFVefRK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C85MAvduJFMrg9MKsfzwry","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 all three are in the same superfamily, a.25.1 'Ferritin-like' (SUNID 47240), within fold a.25 'Ferritin-like' (SUNID 47239) and class a (all alpha). They are separate families: a.25.1.1 Ferritin (SUNID 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (SUNID 47253) and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (SUNID 100951). The same superfamily is SCOPe's claim of probable common ancestry, based on structural and sequence/functional evidence. It is a claim about the shared four-helix-bundle di-metal-site scaffold. The three are different families, so they are more distant than family-level relatives. The superfamily also includes other families such as bacterioferritin-type, YciF-like, half-ferritin, MiaE-like and others. The chemistries differ. Ferritin is a ferroxidase and iron-storage protein that oxidizes Fe(II) with O2 or H2O2 and mineralizes iron in a cage. RNR R2 is a di-iron protein that makes a tyrosyl radical for nucleotide reduction, with some variants using Mn/Fe. Mn catalase has a di-manganese site that disproportionates H2O2. This divergence in function is the expected pattern within a superfamily. A conserved scaffold with a di-metal carboxylate site has been adapted to different metals, redox chemistry and substrates. So the right reading is that the homology is real at the fold/superfamily level, and the chemistry has diverged. Do not conclude from a shared superfamily or a structural match that two proteins share function. Do not transfer a catalytic mechanism, metal specificity, substrate or oligomeric state (24-mer ferritin cage, R1/R2 complex, hexameric catalase) between families without family-level, sequence-level and experimental evidence such as active-site residue conservation and metal binding. Likewise, a hit to this superfamily in a structure or AlphaFold search shows a common fold. It does not show ferritin, RNR or catalase activity. Also, the fold-level grouping in a.25 does not on its own imply homology. Here the homology claim is made at the superfamily level.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/children","pretrained knowledge of ferritin, RNR R2 and Mn catalase chemistry"]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3405,"cache_read_input_tokens":18804,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3405},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32023f22-d1ce-497a-a9d7-235619e61296","uuid":"091e38c8-40d3-4c54-8e58-d654761316b6","timestamp":"2026-10-12T23:22:39.844Z","request_id":"req_011CfyCjQzRKDwNo26zzcfx1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01C85MAvduJFMrg9MKsfzwry":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 all three are in the same superfamily, a.25.1 'Ferritin-like' (SUNID 47240), within fold a.25 'Ferritin-like' (SUNID 47239) and class a (all alpha). They are separate families: a.25.1.1 Ferritin (SUNID 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (SUNID 47253) and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (SUNID 100951). The same superfamily is SCOPe's claim of probable common ancestry, based on structural and sequence/functional evidence. It is a claim about the shared four-helix-bundle di-metal-site scaffold. The three are different families, so they are more distant than family-level relatives. The superfamily also includes other families such as bacterioferritin-type, YciF-like, half-ferritin, MiaE-like and others. The chemistries differ. Ferritin is a ferroxidase and iron-storage protein that oxidizes Fe(II) with O2 or H2O2 and mineralizes iron in a cage. RNR R2 is a di-iron protein that makes a tyrosyl radical for nucleotide reduction, with some variants using Mn/Fe. Mn catalase has a di-manganese site that disproportionates H2O2. This divergence in function is the expected pattern within a superfamily. A conserved scaffold with a di-metal carboxylate site has been adapted to different metals, redox chemistry and substrates. So the right reading is that the homology is real at the fold/superfamily level, and the chemistry has diverged. Do not conclude from a shared superfamily or a structural match that two proteins share function. Do not transfer a catalytic mechanism, metal specificity, substrate or oligomeric state (24-mer ferritin cage, R1/R2 complex, hexameric catalase) between families without family-level, sequence-level and experimental evidence such as active-site residue conservation and metal binding. Likewise, a hit to this superfamily in a structure or AlphaFold search shows a common fold. It does not show ferritin, RNR or catalase activity. Also, the fold-level grouping in a.25 does not on its own imply homology. Here the homology claim is made at the superfamily level.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/children","pretrained knowledge of ferritin, RNR R2 and Mn catalase chemistry"]}]}}}
